2186045417] [17592186567012 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566929 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567012 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566942 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567012 #edb/ref 1069 "q-256700" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567012 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567012 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[-1,-4]\\times [1,4]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-4 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nCalculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts at ${x}_{0}=[-1,\\,4]^{T}$ if the first iteration of the algorithm gives ${x}_{1}=[-2.5,\\,2.5]^{T}.$\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}_{2}=[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-1}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$]$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-2}}\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567012 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "085133b9-a034-5d48-855d-a7e2bd43acae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567012 #edb/ref 1074 "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-4) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-4) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4x & -2y \\\\ 2x & 2y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}4x & -2y \\\\ 2x & 2y\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-4 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-{[\\begin{aligned}4x & -2y \\\\ 2x & 2y\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-4 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2.5 \\\\ 2.5\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}4(-2.5) & -2(2.5) \\\\ 2(-2.5) & 2(2.5)\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}2(-2.5)^{2}-(2.5)^{2}-4 \\\\ (-2.5)^{2}+(2.5)^{2}-8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2.5 \\\\ 2.5\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}-10 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 5\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}2.25 \\\\ 4.5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2.5 \\\\ 2.5\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{(-10)\\cdot 5-(-5)\\cdot (-5)}[\\begin{aligned}5 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -10\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2.25 \\\\ 4.5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2.5 \\\\ 2.5\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{75}[\\begin{aligned}33.75 \\\\ -33.75\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2.05 \\\\ 2.05\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567023 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566928 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567023 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566975 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567023 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566977 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567023 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566996 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567023 #edb/ref 1069 "q-256585" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567023 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567023 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[-3,-1]\\times [1,3]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}x{y}^{3}-y+7 \\\\ {x}^{3}-xy+1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFill in the blanks in the matrix equation below that defines the first iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting  at the point $[-1,\\,2]^{T},$ and compute the components of the first iteration, rounding your final answer to three decimal places (if needed)