Raphson process starting  at the point $[1,\\,1]^{T}.$\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">{{field-1}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})\\,=[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-3}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$].$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n$3$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567040 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "54fa8a66-cee0-5c97-b60e-5706160e8699" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567040 #edb/ref 1074 "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nEquivalently, at each iteration, the above equation can be re-written as\n\n$$\n{J}_{f}({x}_{n})({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-f({x}_{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{3}-2) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{3}-2)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6x & 2y \\\\ {y}^{3} & 3x{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson system of equations is\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}6x & 2y \\\\ {y}^{3} & 3x{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4 \\\\ x{y}^{3}-2\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}6x & 2y \\\\ {y}^{3} & 3x{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}