body></table>\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}_{1}≈[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$]$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n$0.786$\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567058 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "895909c1-3f1c-55f2-a7ef-724fd328cb42" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567058 #edb/ref 1074 "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nEquivalently, at each iteration, the above equation can be re-written as \n\n$$\n{J}_{f}({x}_{n})({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-f({x}_{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{5}-y+10) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{5}-y+10) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy-{y}^{2}+3) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy-{y}^{2}+3)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{4} & -1 \\\\ y & x-2y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson system of equations is\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{4} & -1 \\\\ y & x-2y\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{5}-y+10 \\\\ xy-{y}^{2}+3\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{4} & -1 \\\\ y & x-2y\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{5}-y+10 \\\\ xy-{y}^{2}+3\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}5(-1)^{4} & -1 \\\\ 1 & (-1)-2(1)\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-[\\begin{aligned}(-1)^{5}-(1)+10 \\\\ (-1)(1)-{1}^{2}+3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system of equations, we get $({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{23}{14} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{14}\\end{aligned}].$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}+[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{23}{14} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{14}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{23}{14} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{14}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{37}{14} \\\\ \\frac{11}{14}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}-2.643 \\\\ 0.786\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567063 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566912 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567063 #edb/ref 1069 "q-256606" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567063 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567063 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[0,2]\\times [0,2]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}-{y}^{3}+{x}^{2}y-3 \\\\ {x}^{3}y+{y}^{4}-4x+2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nCalculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts at ${x}_{0}=[1,\\,0]^{T}$ if the first iteration of the algorithm gives ${x}_{1}=[1,\\,2]^{T}.$ Round your final answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}_{1}≈[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-1}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$]$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">$1.497$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567063 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "977b78e7-1471-5b4e-b2ae-73fdfb213d29" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567063 #edb/ref 1074 "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{4}-{y}^{3}+{x}^{2}y-3) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{4}-{y}^{3}+{x}^{2}y-3) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}y+{y}^{4}-4x+2) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}y+{y}^{4}-4x+2)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3}+2xy & -3{y}^{2}+{x}^{2} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}y-4 & {x}^{3}+4{y}^{3}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3}+2xy & -3{y}^{2}+{x}^{2} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}y-4 & {x}^{3}+4{y}^{3}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}-{y}^{3}+{x}^{2}y-3 \\\\ {x}^{3}y+{y}^{4}-4x+2\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-{[\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3}+2xy & -3{y}^{2}+{x}^{2} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}y-4 & {x}^{3}+4{y}^{3}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}-{y}^{3}+{x}^{2}y-3 \\\\ {x}^{3}y+{y}^{4}-4x+2\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}}. \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}4(1)^{3}+2(1)(2) & -3(2)^{2}+{1}^{2} \\\\ 3(1)^{2}(2)-4 & {1}^{3}+4(2)^{3}\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}{1}^{4}-{2}^{3}+(1)^{2}(2)-3 \\\\ (1)^{3}(2)+{2}^{4}-4(1)+2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}8 & -11 \\\\ 2 & 33\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}]. \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{8\\cdot 33-(-11)\\cdot 2}[\\begin{aligned}33 & 11 \\\\ -2 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{286}[\\begin{aligned}-88 \\\\ 144\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}1.308 \\\\ 1.497\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567067 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566946 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567067 #edb/ref 1069 "q-256532" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567067 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567067 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[-3,-2]\\times [-3,0]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-10 \\\\ {y}^{3}-3x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nCalculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts with ${x}_{0}=[-2,\\,-2]^{T}.$ Round your answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}_{1}≈[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-1}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$]$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">$-1.967$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567067 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9f2f93f2-7657-5014-9280-e3a013d49cc7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567067 #edb/ref 1074 "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-10) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-10) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{3}-3x) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{3}-3x)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2x & 2y \\\\ -3 & 3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}2x & 2y \\\\ -3 & 3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-10 \\\\ {y}^{3}-3x\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-{[\\begin{aligned}2x & 2y \\\\ -3 & 3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-10 \\\\ {y}^{3}-3x\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}2(-2) & 2(-2) \\\\ -3 & 3(-2)^{2}\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}(-2)^{2}+(-2)^{2}-10 \\\\ (-2)^{3}-3(-2)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -4 \\\\ -3 & 12\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{(-4)\\cdot 12-(-4)\\cdot (-3)}[\\begin{aligned}12 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{60}[\\begin{aligned}-32 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{38}{15} \\\\ -\\frac{59}{30}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}-2.533 \\\\ -1.967\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567070 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566947 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567070 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566987 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567070 #edb/ref 1069 "q-256722" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567070 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567070 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[0,3]\\times [-2,2]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-xy-4 \\\\ x-{y}^{3}-1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nCalculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts at ${x}_{0}=[2,\\,0]^{T}$ if the first iteration of the algorithm gives ${x}_{1}=[1,\\,-2]^{T}.$ Round your final answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}_{2}≈[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-1}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$]$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-2}}\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567070 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a2448b07-2d2b-5e97-89d6-b8d2e48ba3dc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567070 #edb/ref 1074 "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-xy-4) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-xy-4) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x-{y}^{3}-1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x-{y}^{3}-1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2x-y & -x \\\\ 1 & -3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}2x-y & -x \\\\ 1 & -3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-xy-4 \\\\ x-{y}^{3}-1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-{[\\begin{aligned}2x-y & -x \\\\ 1 & -3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-xy-4 \\\\ x-{y}^{3}-1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}2(1)-(-2) & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3(-2)^{2}\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}{1}^{2}-(1)(-2)-4 \\\\ 1-(-2)^{3}-1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -12\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{4\\cdot (-12)-(-1)\\cdot 1}[\\begin{aligned}-12 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{47}[\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ 33\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{67}{47} \\\\ -\\frac{61}{47}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}1.426 \\\\ -1.298\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567076 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566953 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567076 #edb/ref 1069 "q-256527" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567076 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567076 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[-2,0]\\times [0,2]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-{y}^{2}+1 \\\\ {y}^{3}-{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nCalculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts with ${x}_{0}=[-1,\\,1]^{T}.$ Round your answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}_{1}≈[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">$-0.769$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$]$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-1}}\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567076 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b0b64b0d-c018-5cb8-8dc3-4328291139b8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567076 #edb/ref 1074 "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}-{y}^{2}+1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}-{y}^{2}+1) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{3}-{x}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{3}-{x}^{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2} & -2y \\\\ -2x & 3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2} & -2y \\\\ -2x & 3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-{y}^{2}+1 \\\\ {y}^{3}-{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-{[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2} & -2y \\\\ -2x & 3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-{y}^{2}+1 \\\\ {y}^{3}-{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}3(-1)^{2} & -2(1) \\\\ -2(-1) & 3(1)^{2}\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}(-1)^{3}-{1}^{2}+1 \\\\ {1}^{3}-(-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 3-2\\cdot (-2)}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{13}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{10}{13} \\\\ \\frac{11}{13}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}-0.769 \\\\ 0.846\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567082 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566982 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567082 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566985 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567082 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566990 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567082 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566991 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567082 #edb/ref 1069 "q-256552" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567082 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567082 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[1,3]\\times [-3,-1]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}-{y}^{2}+1 \\\\ {x}^{2}y-y+5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFill in the blanks in the matrix equation below that defines the first iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting  at the point $[1,\\,-2]^{T},$ and compute the components of the first iteration.\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n$4$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})\\,=[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n$2$\n</td>\n<td align=\"left\" rowspan=\"2\">$]$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n$-4\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">{{field-3}}</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}_{1}≈[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">$2.25$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$]$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-4}}\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567082 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bfc018c2-2bf7-5faf-a17d-6c2e774a7e12" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567082 #edb/ref 1074 "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nEquivalently, at each iteration, the above equation can be re-written as \n\n$$\n{J}_{f}({x}_{n})({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-f({x}_{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{4}-{y}^{2}+1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{4}-{y}^{2}+1) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y-y+5) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y-y+5)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3} & -2y \\\\ 2xy & {x}^{2}-1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson system of equations is\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3} & -2y \\\\ 2xy & {x}^{2}-1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}-{y}^{2}+1 \\\\ {x}^{2}y-y+5\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3} & -2y \\\\ 2xy & {x}^{2}-1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}-{y}^{2}+1 \\\\ {x}^{2}y-y+5\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}4(1)^{3} & -2(-2) \\\\ 2(1)(-2) & {1}^{2}-1\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-[\\begin{aligned}{1}^{4}-(-2)^{2}+1 \\\\ (1)^{2}(-2)-(-2)+5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system of equations, we get $({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})=[\\begin{aligned}1.25 \\\\ -0.75\\end{aligned}].$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}+[\\begin{aligned}1.25 \\\\ -0.75\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1.25 \\\\ -0.75\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2.25 \\\\ -2.75\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567092 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566944 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567092 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566974 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567092 #edb/ref 1069 "q-256550" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567092 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567092 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[-3,0]\\times [-3,0]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}-{y}^{2}+3 \\\\ {x}^{2}y-2x+5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nCalculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts with ${x}_{0}=[-2,\\,-2]^{T}.$ Round your answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}_{1}≈[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n            {{field-1}}\n        </td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$]$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n            {{field-2}}\n        </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567092 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "df6b1269-b576-51fc-8968-9319e6cd6df0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567092 #edb/ref 1074 "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{4}-{y}^{2}+3) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{4}-{y}^{2}+3) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y-2x+5) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y-2x+5)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3} & -2y \\\\ 2xy-2 & {x}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3} & -2y \\\\ 2xy-2 & {x}^{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}-{y}^{2}+3 \\\\ {x}^{2}y-2x+5\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-{[\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3} & -2y \\\\ 2xy-2 & {x}^{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}-{y}^{2}+3 \\\\ {x}^{2}y-2x+5\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}4(-2)^{3} & -2(-2) \\\\ 2(-2)(-2)-2 & (-2)^{2}\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}(-2)^{4}-(-2)^{2}+3 \\\\ (-2)^{2}(-2)-2(-2)+5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}-32 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{(-32)\\cdot 4-4\\cdot 6}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -4 \\\\ -6 & -32\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{152}[\\begin{aligned}56 \\\\ -122\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}-1.632 \\\\ -2.803\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567104 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566931 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567104 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566949 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567104 #edb/ref 1069 "q-256714" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567104 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567104 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[-2,0]\\times [0,2]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-1 \\\\ {x}^{3}-3x{y}^{2}-2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nCalculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts at ${x}_{0}=[-1,\\,1]^{T}$ if the first iteration of the algorithm gives ${x}_{1}=[-1.5,\\,1]^{T}.$ Round your final answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}_{2}≈[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-1}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$]$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-2}}\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567104 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f06ea59f-1a95-56b5-bc69-f41150e63482" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567104 #edb/ref 1074 "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-1) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}-3x{y}^{2}-2) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}-3x{y}^{2}-2)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2x & -2y \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2} & -6xy\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}2x & -2y \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2} & -6xy\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-1 \\\\ {x}^{3}-3x{y}^{2}-2\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-{[\\begin{aligned}2x & -2y \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2} & -6xy\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-1 \\\\ {x}^{3}-3x{y}^{2}-2\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1.5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}2(-1.5) & -2(1) \\\\ 3(-1.5)^{2}-3(1)^{2} & -6(-1.5)(1)\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}(-1.5)^{2}-(1)^{2}-1 \\\\ (-1.5)^{3}-3(-1.5)(1)^{2}-2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1.5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ 3.75 & 9\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}0.25 \\\\ -0.875\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1.5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{(-3)\\cdot 9-(-2)\\cdot 3.75}[\\begin{aligned}9 & 2 \\\\ -3.75 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.25 \\\\ -0.875\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1.5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{19.5}[\\begin{aligned}0.5 \\\\ 1.6875\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}-1.474 \\\\ 1.087\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567109 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566901 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567109 #edb/ref 1069 "q-256530" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567109 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186567109 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[-2,2]\\times [-2,2]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{y}^{3}-1 \\\\ {y}^{2}-2x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nCalculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts with ${x}_{0}=[-1,\\,1]^{T}.$\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=